# b08 — a waiting method (`count`) on a `Child`'s `lines` in a plain
# `def main`: not a `for`, as the example of this message has, but a method
# that waits for the items.
# Meant to FAIL.
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# error: `c.lines` hands out lines as the program writes them, so it is walked in async code
#   --> corpus/m96/b08_lines_count_outside_async.lume:17:8
#    |
# 17 |   n = c.lines.count { |l| l == "a" }
#    |        ^
#   help: walk it in an `async def` or a `spawn:` body; `Process.run` waits for the program and gives all of its output at once

def main -> () or Error:
  (code, out, _) = Process.run("printf", ["a\nb\na\n"])?
  puts "#{code} #{out.lines.len}"
  c = Process.spawn("printf", ["a\nb\na\n"])?
  n = c.lines.count { |l| l == "a" }
  puts n
  ()
